# 1️⃣ Functional Dependency (FD)

## Big Idea

A Functional Dependency describes a relationship between columns.

It answers:

```text id="0b9k7v"
If I know X,
can I determine Y?
```

---

## Real-World Example 🧠

Think about a student.

| student_id | name   |
| ---------- | ------ |
| 101        | Akshay |
| 102        | Rahul  |

If someone gives you:

```text id="0mr3eu"
student_id = 101
```

Can you find the student's name?

✔ Yes

Therefore:

```text id="s5f0wo"
student_id → name
```

This is a Functional Dependency.

---

# Meaning of Arrow (→)

```text id="zykw05"
X → Y
```

means:

```text id="mjhh0l"
X determines Y
```

---

## More Examples

### Aadhaar Number

```text id="2j0yyq"
Aadhaar → Person
```

Because one Aadhaar identifies exactly one person.

---

### Employee ID

```text id="0jql4w"
Employee_ID → Employee_Name
```

---

# What is NOT a Functional Dependency?

Suppose:

| Branch | Student |
| ------ | ------- |
| CSE    | Akshay  |
| CSE    | Rahul   |

Can branch determine student?

❌ No

Because:

```text id="7ewxg4"
CSE → many students
```

Not unique.

---

# Mental Model 🧠

Think:

```text id="q3r7bx"
Primary Key determines everything else
```

Example:

| id | name | branch |
| -- | ---- | ------ |

```text id="9n8m3z"
id → name
id → branch
```

---

# Why Functional Dependency Matters

Normalization is based on identifying:

✔ Good dependencies

❌ Bad dependencies

---

